---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';

const source = 'https://github.com/sangtrx/curren';
const canonical = new URL(withBase('/work/curren'), Astro.site).href;
const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@type': 'Article',
    '@id': `${canonical}#case-study`,
    headline: 'Curren — point-in-time quantitative research and verifiable trading-system distribution',
    description: 'Case study of Curren, an independent AI/quant side project spanning point-in-time market data, hypothesis research, leakage/overfitting controls, production signal lifecycle, guarded execution boundaries, immutable publication proofs, and public API/CLI/MCP interfaces.',
    dateModified: '2026-08-28T14:50:00+07:00',
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Sang Truong', url: new URL(withBase('/about'), Astro.site).href },
    mainEntityOfPage: canonical,
    isBasedOn: source,
    about: ['Quantitative research', 'Point-in-time data', 'Backtesting', 'Out-of-sample validation', 'Trading systems', 'FastAPI', 'MCP', 'AI agents'],
  },
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL(withBase('/'), Astro.site).href },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Curren', item: canonical },
    ],
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Curren — Quant Research, Trading Systems & MCP Case Study | Sang Truong"
  description="Deep technical case study of Sang Truong's independent Curren project: point-in-time market data, hypothesis research, leakage-aware OOS validation, ML selection, signal lifecycle, guarded execution, immutable public proofs, FastAPI, CLI, and MCP."
  image="og-curren.png"
  pageType="article"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="case container section">
    <a class="back-link" href={withBase('#work')}>← Selected work</a>
    <header class="case-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Case Study 02 · Independent Quant / Trading Systems · Jun 2026 — Present</p>
      <h1>Curren</h1>
      <p class="case-lede">A solo-built quantitative research and trading-systems project designed around a full research lifecycle rather than one strategy: point-in-time data, reusable hypothesis generation, disciplined validation, production signal/risk state, guarded execution, and a public verification surface that does not expose private alpha.</p>
      <div class="case-meta">
        <div><span>Role</span><strong>Independent AI/Quant Side Project · Solo Builder</strong></div>
        <div><span>Layers</span><strong>Research · ML selection · lifecycle/risk · API / CLI / MCP</strong></div>
        <div><span>Public status</span><strong>v0.4.0 alpha · live public feed not claimed</strong></div>
      </div>
      <p class="case-verified">Public-platform boundary last verified: <time datetime="2026-08-23">23 Aug 2026</time></p>
      <div class="hero-actions case-actions"><a class="button primary" href={source}>View public source ↗</a></div>
    </header>

    <section class="case-section visual-panel quant-architecture" aria-label="Curren two-layer system architecture">
      <div class="layer-stack">
        <div class="layer private-layer"><small>PRIVATE RESEARCH + TRADING LAYER</small><strong>PIT data → features/hypotheses → OOS validation → ML quality → lifecycle / risk / execution</strong></div>
        <span class="layer-arrow">↓ strict sanitized PublicationBatch</span>
        <div class="layer public-layer"><small>PUBLIC VERIFICATION LAYER</small><strong>immutable read model → API · CLI · MCP · Omarchy</strong></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section case-grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Problem</p><h2>A trading system can look convincing while being methodologically wrong.</h2></div>
      <div class="case-copy">
        <p>A backtest can accidentally use future information. Repeated hypothesis search can turn noise into an impressive chart. A live result can look good even if the original plan can later be rewritten. And a public API can be useful while still leaking strategy or execution state.</p>
        <p>Curren treats research validity, live operational state, and public verification as separate engineering contracts. The goal is to make false confidence harder to manufacture before optimizing headline returns.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section section-rule case-grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">My ownership</p><h2>Research protocol to production and public developer surface.</h2></div>
      <div class="ownership-list">
        <article><strong>Research architecture</strong><p>Designed point-in-time raw-to-canonical lineage, reusable feature/hypothesis generation, event studies/backtests, walk-forward/OOS evaluation, leakage checks, multiple-testing/overfitting diagnostics, experiment tracking, and reproducible research releases.</p></article>
        <article><strong>Modeling & selection</strong><p>Built ML quality/trade-selection layers with LightGBM/CatBoost/XGBoost-class tooling and portfolio/risk evaluation around research outputs instead of optimizing one prediction metric.</p></article>
        <article><strong>Live trading-system state</strong><p>Connected selected outputs to durable signal/lifecycle/P&L state, risk gates, guarded execution, reconciliation, idempotency, restart recovery, and operator controls while keeping live state separate from offline research artifacts.</p></article>
        <article><strong>Product & verification surfaces</strong><p>Built supporting FastAPI/public read-model contracts, CLI/MCP tooling, access/product surfaces, and immutable publication controls without presenting the project as primary employment or claiming validated profitable alpha.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section section-rule">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research methodology</p>
      <div class="research-flow" aria-label="Quant research flow">
        <span>RAW</span><i>→</i><span>PIT CANONICAL</span><i>→</i><span>FEATURES</span><i>→</i><span>HYPOTHESES</span><i>→</i><span>EVENT STUDY</span><i>→</i><span>OOS</span><i>→</i><span>PORTFOLIO</span>
      </div>
      <div class="contract-grid contract-grid-spaced">
        <div class="code-card"><span class="code-comment">// every feature must be knowable at decision time</span><strong>available_time ≤ event_time</strong><span class="code-comment">// research validity starts before model selection</span></div>
        <div class="principle-card"><span>VALIDATION</span><strong>walk-forward / OOS · leakage checks · purging/embargo concepts</strong><span>OVERFITTING CONTROL</span><strong>multiple testing · robustness · reproducible releases · holdout discipline</strong></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section section-rule case-grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Research data & modeling</p><h2>A reusable research environment, not a single notebook strategy.</h2></div>
      <div class="capability-grid">
        <article><h3>Data lineage</h3><p>Point-in-time market data, immutable raw history, canonical Parquet-oriented datasets, repeatable releases, and explicit availability-time rules.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Feature & hypothesis factory</h3><p>Reusable factor/event families across price, volume, market structure and derived signals rather than one hard-coded strategy path.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Modeling</h3><p>LightGBM, CatBoost, XGBoost, Scikit-learn and supporting numerical/statistical tooling for quality selection and research diagnostics.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Research engineering</h3><p>Polars/Pandas, PyArrow/Parquet, DuckDB, NumPy, SciPy, Statsmodels, Numba, Optuna and experiment/release discipline.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section section-rule">
      <p class="eyebrow">Public distribution layer</p>
      <div class="decision-grid decision-grid-3">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>One-way publication</h3><p>The private runtime publishes a strict sanitized projection. Public clients never connect directly to the trading database or execution controls.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Immutable initial plan</h3><p>The first accepted signal plan is hashed and retained so entry, stop, targets, side, and publication context cannot be silently rewritten later.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Append-only lifecycle</h3><p>Lifecycle identities are append-only, conflict-checked, and protected by per-signal source timestamps so stale retries cannot roll state backward.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Frozen terminal outcome</h3><p>Terminal results and proof-backed track record derive only from immutable outcome records, not mutable presentation state.</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Entitlement-aware visibility</h3><p>Public active context can be delayed/hidden while Premium and Agent views receive only allowed fields; server-side policy enforces the minimum visibility boundary.</p></article>
        <article><span>06</span><h3>Read-only agent interface</h3><p>MCP exposes six read-only tools. Remote unauthenticated MCP is deliberately refused; no public tool places orders or changes trading controls.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section section-rule case-grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Public developer surface</p><h2>One read model, multiple clients.</h2></div>
      <div class="capability-grid">
        <article><h3>FastAPI</h3><p>Summary, signals, lifecycle, results, track record, and verification endpoints over a public read model.</p></article>
        <article><h3>CLI</h3><p>Human-readable and JSON access to signals, lifecycle, results, track record, and verification without reconstructing hidden fields.</p></article>
        <article><h3>MCP</h3><p>Six read-only tools for MCP-compatible AI agents to inspect active signals, lifecycle, results, track record, and proofs.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Omarchy</h3><p>A native Quattro widget/panel using delayed anonymous/public context only, with no private execution capability embedded in the client.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section section-rule">
      <p class="eyebrow">Current public-platform status · v0.4.0 alpha</p>
      <div class="status-table" role="table" aria-label="Curren public platform status">
        <div role="row"><span role="cell">API / SQLite-WAL read model / entitlements</span><strong role="cell" class="status-ok">Implemented</strong></div>
        <div role="row"><span role="cell">CLI / async client / read-only MCP / Omarchy plugin</span><strong role="cell" class="status-ok">Implemented</strong></div>
        <div role="row"><span role="cell">Immutable plan / lifecycle / terminal proof controls</span><strong role="cell" class="status-ok">Implemented</strong></div>
        <div role="row"><span role="cell">Private runtime publication projector</span><strong role="cell" class="status-wip">Pending integration</strong></div>
        <div role="row"><span role="cell">api.curren.tech production deployment</span><strong role="cell" class="status-wip">Pending</strong></div>
        <div role="row"><span role="cell">Live public signal feed / validated profitable alpha</span><strong role="cell">Not claimed</strong></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section demo-placeholder">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Public evidence boundary</p>
        <h2>Expose proofs, not private alpha.</h2>
        <p>The public repository intentionally excludes private strategy/research logic, raw-source ingestion, AI guard internals, exchange credentials, execution runtime, accounts, and the production trading database. The portfolio can show methodology, architecture, historical evidence, public contracts, and verification behavior without publishing the edge itself.</p>
      </div>
      <a class="button ghost" href={source}>GitHub source ↗</a>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
